{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T16:38:55.811Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ada30d9ef6a7eb8c7</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_017n4Ka7zygCmTP7oU8j3AuA</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/ada30d9ef6a7eb8c7.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Re-audit Track 1 spec after redraft\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I read the spec fresh from disk along with the live source it references — `crates/callisto-graph/src/groups.rs`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs`, and `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/snapshot.rs` — plus the other `.claude/specs/` files to check for scope overlap. The revised AC-001/AC-002 wiring claims (line numbers, function signatures, error variants, sort/dedup mechanics) check out against the live code. Four real issues survived adversarial review; overall verdict is **fail**.\n\nThe central judgment call the task asked me to make on evidence, not assumption: I traced `fixed_group_target`, `pre_mutation_checks`, and the new/existing convergence blocks in `solve_cascade` line by line for what happens if a group's `members` Vec contains the same `PackageId` twice (two spellings). In every one of these paths, the aggregation is keyed by `PackageId` and is either a `max()` over repeated equal values, an equality-gated `insert` (`if out.targets.get(&amp;id) != Some(&amp;winner)`), or feeds a `BTreeSet` worklist — all naturally idempotent under a repeated key. A duplicated member does not corrupt `AC-001`/`AC-002`'s target computation or `AC-007`'s divergence detection; it only causes harmless redundant iteration. That contradicts the premise embedded in AC-010c's justification, so I'm treating the dedup requirement as scope creep the drafter rationalized into self-consistency (by amending non_goals) rather than a necessity proven from the other criteria.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-010c\",\n      \"type\": \"scope-overlap\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-010c, the api_surface `.dedup()` addition to GroupTable::resolve, and the non_goals item 4 exception all rest on the claim that leaving duplicate same-group member spellings undeduplicated would corrupt AC-001/AC-002's fixed_group_target computation and AC-007's divergence detection (both iterate g.members(...)). I traced the actual code: fixed_group_target's `released` list is filtered to tagged members only and only released[0] is read (a duplicate spelling of an untagged sibling never enters `released`); its max_sev loop is `if s &gt; max_sev { max_sev = s }`, idempotent under a repeated id/severity pair; pre_mutation_checks's `pairs` comparison is also idempotent because a duplicate entry for the same PackageId carries the identical base version, so `Version::compare(v, first_v)` never flips `divergent`; and the new/existing convergence blocks in solve_cascade key their `out.targets`/`out.severities` writes by PackageId through an equality-gated insert into a BTreeSet-backed worklist, which collapses repeated keys. None of AC-001/AC-002/AC-004/AC-007 can be corrupted by an undeduplicated duplicate member under the code as written today. The revision_note's fix (amending non_goals item 4 with a carve-out exception, and adding `.dedup()` to the api_surface entry) makes the document internally self-consistent on paper, but self-consistency is not the same as grounding — the canon-verifier's flag that AC-010c traces to a prior audit gap rather than the source requirement's done_when list stands unrebutted. This is real, additive scope creep bolted onto a bug-fix spec via a self-authored non_goals exception, not a necessary correctness precondition.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Remove AC-010c from acceptance_criteria entirely. In the `config::groups::GroupTable::resolve` api_surface entry, delete point (2) in its entirety (the `.dedup()` addition and its justifying prose) and keep only point (1), the cross-group conflict detection. Restore non_goals item 4 to its original, unqualified form: 'GroupTable::resolve's other existing responsibilities and error paths beyond the new ConflictingGroupMembership detection — do not refactor unrelated parts of resolve() as a side effect.' Then add a NEW, separate non_goals item: 'Deduplicating a group's own member list when the same PackageId is reachable under two different spellings within one group (e.g. \\\"my-lib\\\" and \\\"cargo:my-lib\\\") is explicitly out of scope for this track. Verified against the live code: fixed_group_target's alignment-base lookup only reads released[0] from a tag-filtered subset, its max_sev computation is idempotent over repeated (id, severity) pairs, pre_mutation_checks's grammar/divergence comparison is idempotent over repeated (id, version) pairs, and solve_cascade's convergence blocks (both the pre-existing Linked-group block and the new Fixed-group block) write out.targets/out.severities through an equality-gated insert into a BTreeSet-backed worklist that naturally collapses a repeated PackageId key — so an undeduplicated duplicate spelling cannot corrupt AC-001, AC-002, AC-004, or AC-007. Deduplication remains a reasonable follow-up cleanup (SPEC-TRACK1B-GROUP-MEMBER-DEDUP) but is not required for this track's correctness contract and must not be smuggled in as a precondition for a differently-scoped criterion.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-003\",\n      \"type\": \"incomplete\",\n      \"description\": \"The scope section and AC-003 both restrict the CascadeInput-field-addition compilation check to commands/version.rs and commands/snapshot.rs. But cascade.rs's own `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` block constructs CascadeInput directly at seven sites (verified: lines 698, 779, 850, 947, 1074, 1157, 1237), none of which supply a `tags` field. Adding `tags: &amp;'a TagIndex` as a required (non-Optional) field breaks compilation of all seven once this spec lands, but AC-003's wording ('no compilation errors referencing either file') is scoped so narrowly that it would technically still 'pass' while cascade.rs's test module fails to build — since the resulting compiler errors reference cascade.rs, not version.rs or snapshot.rs. AC-003 also says `cargo build -p callisto-graph`, which does not even compile `#[cfg(test)]` code, so it would report success while `cargo test` fails.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Expand the scope entry for cascade.rs to read: 'crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs (CascadeInput struct gains a `tags` field; solve_cascade gains a new Fixed-group convergence block ...; additionally, all seven pre-existing CascadeInput literals inside cascade.rs's own `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` block (currently missing a `tags` field, e.g. the literal at line 698) must be updated to supply `tags: &amp;TagIndex::empty()`, or a populated TagIndex where the specific test's Fixed-group scenario requires real tag data, since this struct-field addition is required, not optional, and breaks their compilation otherwise).' Rewrite AC-003 to: 'crates/callisto-graph builds and its test suite compiles successfully after CascadeInput gains a `tags: &amp;TagIndex` field, with every CascadeInput construction site updated to supply it: commands/version.rs's plan_version (line 70), commands/snapshot.rs's plan_snapshot (line 53), and all seven pre-existing CascadeInput literals inside cascade.rs's own `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` block. `cargo test -p callisto-graph --no-run` (or the workspace equivalent via `just ci` / `moon run :test`) completes with zero compilation errors in any of these three files — cascade.rs's test module included, not just version.rs and snapshot.rs.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-003\",\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"The scope section calls commands/snapshot.rs's CascadeInput update 'mechanical update only.' It is not: to supply the new `tags` field, plan_snapshot must call `ws.tags()`, which it does not call anywhere today. `ws.tags()` (crates/callisto-graph/src/lib.rs:148) is fallible — it calls `TagIndex::build(self.git_access(), &amp;self.graph, &amp;self.config)?` on first access and can return Err(GraphError). This is a brand-new failure surface introduced into plan_snapshot's call path by this spec's wiring, and no acceptance criterion states what plan_snapshot should do when it fails (propagate the Err? swallow it? panic?). AC-005/AC-006 describe plan_snapshot's success-path output only.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Rewrite the snapshot.rs scope entry to: 'crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/snapshot.rs (CascadeInput construction at line 53 gains a `tags` argument via a new `let tags = ws.tags()?;` call; this is not purely mechanical — plan_snapshot does not call ws.tags() anywhere today, so this introduces a new fallible code path into plan_snapshot's error surface; see AC-014 for its contract).' Add a new acceptance criterion: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"AC-014\\\", \\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Given a workspace where ws.tags() (TagIndex::build) returns Err(_) — for example because git tag data cannot be read via the workspace's git_access() — running commands::snapshot::plan_snapshot propagates that same Err as its own Err result, and no VersionPlan or SnapshotReport is produced. A test forces TagIndex::build to fail (e.g. via a CommandRunner stub that errors on the git tag-listing call) and asserts plan_snapshot returns Err rather than panicking or silently proceeding with an empty/partial TagIndex.\\\", \\\"is_error_case\\\": true}.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-008\",\n      \"type\": \"incomplete\",\n      \"description\": \"GroupCheckOutcome has two fields: new_members and diagnostics (crates/callisto-graph/src/groups.rs:12-16). pre_mutation_checks populates new_members with each Fixed group's not-yet-released ('fresh') members whenever that list is non-empty (groups.rs:84-86). Grepping the workspace confirms new_members is never read anywhere in the codebase today. This spec's plan_version api_surface entry (d) wires up only `.diagnostics` ('merging of the returned GroupCheckOutcome.diagnostics into the existing diagnostics collection'); it says nothing about new_members. After this spec lands, new_members remains permanently write-only — populated by a real, now-reachable call site, but never consumed by anything. The spec neither states this is intentional (no non_goals entry) nor gives it a criterion, leaving a reader unable to tell whether the omission is a deliberate scope boundary or a missed requirement.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add a new non_goals item: 'GroupCheckOutcome.new_members (the list of a Fixed group's not-yet-released members) is populated by pre_mutation_checks but intentionally not consumed by this spec's plan_version wiring — only GroupCheckOutcome.diagnostics is merged into VersionPlan.diagnostics. Surfacing new_members (e.g. as a diagnostic or changelog annotation marking a Fixed-group sibling's first release) is a distinct, additive behavior with no basis in REQ-TRACK1-FIXED-GROUP-CASCADE-CORRECTNESS's done_when list and is deferred to a follow-up track. This spec's wiring leaves new_members write-only by design, not by oversight.' If new_members surfacing was in fact intended, instead add: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"AC-015\\\", \\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Given a pre_mutation_checks call whose returned GroupCheckOutcome.new_members is non-empty for some group, running commands::version::plan_version produces a VersionPlan whose diagnostics field includes one Diagnostic per group listed in new_members, with &lt;exact DiagnosticCode and message format to be specified&gt;.\\\", \\\"is_error_case\\\": false} — but this alternative requires a concrete DiagnosticCode/message contract the auditor cannot supply without additional domain input, so if this path is chosen it must be authored by the spec owner, not guessed.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"Verified every line number, signature, and error-variant claim in the spec against live source (groups.rs, config/groups.rs, cascade.rs, napi.rs, version.rs, snapshot.rs, error.rs, report.rs) rather than trusting the document's paraphrase; all checked out. The AC-001/AC-002 redraft is internally consistent with the new Fixed-group-convergence-block framing and correctly reflects the code (BTreeSet worklist, equality-gated insert, released[0] alignment base). The one issue the task specifically asked me to weigh — AC-010c's grounding — I resolved by tracing the actual dependent computations rather than accepting the spec's self-declared consistency: the dedup requirement is not load-bearing for any other criterion in this spec and should be split out. Three further concrete gaps surfaced during the standard five-dimension pass: an AC-003 compile-check loophole that would pass while breaking cascade.rs's own test module, an undocumented new fallible path introduced into plan_snapshot, and a newly-reachable-but-never-consumed GroupCheckOutcome field. No untestable/vague language, no unresolved TBDs, and no overlap with other workspace specs (Track3A/B and Track G touch disjoint files) were found.\"\n}\n```\n\nFiles read for this audit (all absolute paths):\n- `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118/scratchpad/spec-track1-draft.json`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/groups.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/snapshot.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/error.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/report.rs`\n- `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/*.json` (scope-overlap check)</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>87436</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>25</tool_uses><duration_ms>1053784</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
