{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T20:59:05.344Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9a8edbd032839ab1</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/a9a8edbd032839ab1.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit Track 2 spec quality\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Confirmed the error shape matches AC-012's claim. Here is the audit result.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-002\",\n      \"type\": \"ambiguous\",\n      \"description\": \"The criterion says PlatformWrite.from is read 'via the platform manifest's own current_version()' but never specifies which ManifestRole plan_version must pass into ManifestDecl::new when opening path P. This is not a cosmetic gap: GroupMember::PlatformManifest carries its own `role: ManifestRole` field (crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs:28-33), and existing code (crates/callisto-graph/src/groups.rs:186-199) genuinely populates that field with ManifestRole::Platform{platform, arch, abi} for real platform-package members. ManifestDecl::new (crates/callisto-model/src/package.rs:88-107) actively rejects that combination: `if matches!(role, ManifestRole::Platform{..}) &amp;&amp; format == ManifestFormat::CargoToml { return Err(invalid_role_for_format) }`. So an implementer who does the 'obvious' thing -- thread the GroupMember's own role field through to open the manifest at plan time -- will get a spurious construction error for any Cargo/TOML-formatted platform manifest (a real maturin/Rust platform-package layout, the same class of scenario AC-007 calls out for napi-rs). A second implementer who instead hardcodes ManifestRole::Canonical (mirroring how apply.rs's existing platform_writes dispatch loop already opens the exact same path at apply.rs:247, and optional_dep_updates at apply.rs:257) will not hit this and will produce different, correct behavior. Two competent implementers reading AC-002 as written will diverge on a Cargo-manifest platform package -- one crashes plan_version with a bogus error, the other doesn't. This is precisely the underspecified-mechanism gap that caused Track 1's first exit-gate failure.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Replace AC-002 with: 'In the AC-001 scenario, PlatformWrite.from equals the version string currently declared in the platform manifest file at path P on disk at plan time. plan_version reads P via ManifestFormat::from_path(P) to detect format, then ManifestDecl::new(P, ManifestRole::Canonical, format) to open it and call current_version() -- ManifestRole::Canonical is used unconditionally for this read, regardless of the value of the owning GroupMember::PlatformManifest's own `role` field, so that a Cargo/TOML-formatted platform manifest (e.g. a maturin platform crate) opens successfully rather than tripping ManifestDecl::new's Platform-role-vs-CargoToml-format rejection (crates/callisto-model/src/package.rs:104-106). This mirrors the ManifestRole::Canonical construction already used by apply_version_plan's platform_writes and optional_dep_updates dispatch loops (crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs:247,257) when opening these same paths, so plan-time and apply-time reads of a given platform manifest use the same role and cannot diverge in how ManifestDecl validates it.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-012\",\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"Fix 2's api_surface entry claims the new precondition check 'mirrors the existing bump loops' (apply.rs:146-161, 190-200), which use a three-way branch: current==target -&gt; no-op success (retry-safe idempotence), current==from -&gt; write, else -&gt; UnexpectedManifestVersion error. AC-012 and AC-013 as written only specify two of those three branches (the error-on-mismatch case and the ordinary from-matches case) -- neither criterion states what happens when current_version() already equals PlatformWrite.version (i.e. a crashed apply is retried after the platform manifest was already written but before the whole apply completed). Read literally, AC-012's condition is 'from does not match on-disk current_version()' -- which is also true in the idempotent-retry case, since current now equals `version`, not `from`. An implementer following AC-012's literal text would return UnexpectedManifestVersion on a legitimate retry, breaking idempotent apply for platform manifests specifically -- the one area track-b-idempotent-apply.json's scope explicitly excludes (its scope is limited to the VersionWriteTarget::Manifest arm and consumed_changesets), so this spec is the only place that idempotency for platform_writes gets decided, and as written it decides it incorrectly.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add a new criterion AC-012b: 'Fix 2 idempotent-retry case: given a VersionPlan.platform_writes entry whose `from` field does NOT match the platform manifest's on-disk current_version(), but that on-disk current_version() already equals PlatformWrite.version (the platform manifest was already written to its target by a prior, interrupted apply run), calling apply_version_plan treats this as success -- it does not write the manifest again and does not return UnexpectedManifestVersion, but still appends the manifest path to modified_paths so it is re-staged on retry.' Then narrow AC-012 by appending: '...this criterion applies only when the on-disk current_version() equals neither PlatformWrite.from nor PlatformWrite.version -- see AC-012b for the current==version idempotent-retry case, which is not an error.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-007\",\n      \"type\": \"incomplete\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-007 establishes that platform_writes gets one PlatformWrite entry per sibling GroupMember::PlatformManifest when an owner has multiple platform targets (e.g. linux-x64-gnu and darwin-arm64), but no criterion states the corresponding shape of optional_dep_updates in that same multi-sibling scenario. If the owner's canonical manifest has matching optionalDependencies entries for both platform names (AC-003's precondition, satisfied twice), should VersionPlan.optional_dep_updates contain one OptionalDepUpdate for the owner's manifest with both (name, version) pairs merged into `updates`, or two separate OptionalDepUpdate entries that both name the same manifest path? This matters operationally, not just stylistically: apply_version_plan's optional_dep_updates dispatch loop (apply.rs lines 254-262, explicitly excluded from SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002's batching per this spec's non_goals) opens, mutates, and persists the manifest handle once per loop iteration with no path-deduplication -- two entries for the same manifest path means two independent open/parse/persist(atomic_write) cycles against the same file in the same apply run, and modified_paths would contain the same path twice. Two implementers following AC-003+AC-007 without a merge rule will produce genuinely different runtime behavior (one atomic write vs. two sequential ones touching the same file), not just different code shape.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add a new criterion AC-007b: 'Given the AC-007 scenario (owner with two GroupMember::PlatformManifest siblings, both bumped) where the owner's canonical manifest has matching optionalDependencies entries for both platform names, plan_version's VersionPlan.optional_dep_updates contains exactly one OptionalDepUpdate entry for the owner's canonical manifest path, whose `updates` field contains both (name, version) pairs -- entries are merged per target manifest path, never duplicated as separate OptionalDepUpdate entries pointing at the same path.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-014\",\n      \"type\": \"untestable\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-014 requires that every other criterion be 'proven through the real, live call path... not through an isolated unit test that calls a helper function directly.' This describes a constraint on how the test suite is structured, not an externally observable behavior of the system -- a tester with no knowledge of the test implementation cannot confirm or refute it by observing plan_version's or apply_version_plan's output, only by reading the test file's call graph. It is unambiguous as written (so it is not blocking on its own), but it is a testing-process rule rather than a falsifiable proposition about system behavior, and mixing it into acceptance_criteria alongside AC-001 through AC-013 blurs the line the other 13 criteria correctly hold.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Move this requirement out of acceptance_criteria and into a dedicated top-level `testing_constraints` note (or into non_goals as a stated exclusion of helper-level-only tests), e.g.: 'All tests for AC-001 through AC-013 must invoke commands::version::plan_version or apply::apply_version_plan directly as their entry point; a test that only calls an internal helper function and bypasses one of these two public entry points does not satisfy its corresponding criterion.' If it must remain a numbered criterion for tooling reasons, keep it, but do not let it stand as a peer of the other externally-observable criteria in the pass/fail count.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"Verified the 4 canon-flagged additions against live code: AC-004 (negative optionalDependencies case) and AC-007 (multi-platform cardinality for platform_writes) are clean, well-specified, and legitimate completeness additions with no issues. AC-002 and AC-008 are legitimate in spirit (a `from` field needs a defined derivation, and a malformed-manifest error path is a real gap) but AC-002's derivation mechanism is underspecified in exactly the way that caused Track 1's exit-gate failure: GroupMember::PlatformManifest carries its own ManifestRole::Platform{..} field (confirmed live at groups.rs:186-199), and ManifestDecl::new (package.rs:104-106) actively rejects Platform-role+CargoToml-format combinations -- so the 'obvious' implementation (thread the group member's own role through) breaks for Cargo-format platform manifests, while the alternative (hardcode ManifestRole::Canonical, matching apply.rs's existing convention at apply.rs:247,257) doesn't. This is a genuine two-valid-readings ambiguity with different runtime outcomes, not a style nit. Independently found: AC-012 (Fix 2's precondition check) omits the idempotent-retry branch (current on-disk version already equals the target) that the sibling bump loops it claims to mirror actually implement, which would make a legitimate crash-retry throw UnexpectedManifestVersion -- a missing error/edge case with real correctness impact, and notably the one place idempotency for platform_writes gets decided since track-b-idempotent-apply.json explicitly excludes this loop from its own scope. AC-003+AC-007 leave the multi-platform-sibling shape of optional_dep_updates unspecified, which has a concrete runtime consequence given apply.rs's un-batched optional_dep_updates loop (duplicate opens/persists of the same manifest path). AC-014 is a minor testing-process criterion masquerading as an acceptance criterion. Scope-overlap check: grepped SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json and track-b-idempotent-apply.json for platform_writes/optional_dep_updates/active_ecosystems -- zero hits in either; APPLY-BATCH-002's batching pass explicitly restructures only the bumps/rewrites loops and this spec's own non_goals correctly disclaims touching classify_manifest_writes; track-b's scope is limited to the VersionWriteTarget::Manifest arm and consumed_changesets. No real overlap found on any of the three referenced sibling specs. Verdict is fail because AC-002 and AC-012 are not cosmetic -- as written, a competent implementer following the letter of the spec produces code with a real correctness bug (broken retry) or a real crash on a realistic input (Cargo-format platform manifest), which is exactly the standard this audit exists to catch.\"\n}\n```\n\nKey files read/inspected for this audit: `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118/scratchpad/spec-track2-draft.json`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/plan.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/apply.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/groups.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-model/src/package.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/track-b-idempotent-apply.json`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK1-FIXED-GROUP-CASCADE-CORRECTNESS.json`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>70192</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>12</tool_uses><duration_ms>203357</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
