{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:11:50.916Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a775218dae24a4a1d</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_013RDVcV5nMjtDe7n8VtDj3b</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/a775218dae24a4a1d.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Research match semantics docs and GH Action JSON parsing\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Q1 — Match semantics.** Docs are unambiguous and internally consistent that `match` is PATH-based, not just an isolated example:\n\n- `docs/00-design.md:1367,1379,1384`: worked example uses `match = \"crates/*\"`, `match = \"packages/*\"`, `match = \"packages/special-case\"` — real path-glob patterns, deliberately paired in the same example with NAME-based `members = [\"foo\", \"foo-cli\"]` for `[[fixed-group]]`/`[[linked-group]]` (so the doc distinguishes \"match = path\" from \"members = name\" on purpose, not by accident).\n- `docs/01-spec.md:6331-6334` — §14's own formal algorithm: `if an explicit [[package]] whose match == P` and `for each [[package-set]] whose match glob ⊇ P`, where P is explicitly defined as \"each discovered package P (by project root path, workspace-relative)\" (line 6325). This is the spec's normative pseudocode, not just illustrative prose.\n- No real (non-illustrative) config anywhere in the repo uses path-style patterns — the only hits are this one worked example.\n\nThe REAL shipped code (`config/pattern.rs`, `config/resolve.rs`) already does something different and coherent: `PackagePattern` matches by NAME glob with an optional `ecosystem:` prefix for disambiguation (`\"cargo:pkg-*\"` vs `\"npm:pkg-*\"`), backed by `PackageId::matches`, with a dedicated error (`BareRuleMatchesMultipleEcosystems`, `docs/01-spec.md:1572-1576`) telling the user to add an ecosystem prefix when a bare name-pattern is ambiguous across ecosystems. This is tested, working, and structurally exactly \"name + publish-method(ecosystem) uniqueness.\"\n\n**Q2 — GitHub Action JSON dependency.** Only two real `jq` sites parse callisto's own JSON output (as opposed to `gh` CLI output, which is separate and unaffected):\n- `action.yml:164`: `callisto matrix --format json | jq -c '[.platformTargets[].targets[]] | unique_by(.artifactName)'` — reads `MatrixReport.platformTargets[].targets[].artifactName`.\n- `action.yml:202`: `echo \"$PLAN_JSON\" | jq -r '.releases[]?.tagName // empty'` — reads a `.releases[].tagName` field off `callisto plan-publish --format json`'s output, also passed opaquely to `callisto tag --plan \"$PLAN_JSON\"`.\n\nEverything else is exit-code or CLI-invocation based, not structural JSON parsing: `callisto status --check` (line 87) branches purely on `$?` (0/1/2), no JSON read at all; `callisto publish`, `callisto tag`, `git push` are shelled out to directly with no output parsing. Confirmed independently: this exit-code branch is already broken as shipped — it treats exit 0 as \"has changesets,\" but the documented/real contract is 1=pending/2=none, so that branch is currently unreachable in real runs, a pre-existing bug unrelated to the JSON-shape question.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>805999</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>7</tool_uses><duration_ms>100793</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
