{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:15:44.643Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae20d743524e992a0</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_014vdXJZ1FsCA7CsyJsD7qSG</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/ae20d743524e992a0.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Challenge Track 9 rewrite-key plan\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Confirmed the pattern for the first 7 sites. Final answer below.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"T2\",\n      \"type\": \"under-specified\",\n      \"description\": \"T2's mechanical-fixup step for the 8 in-file CascadeInput test literals in crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs asserts 'every one of the 8 existing CascadeInput literals ends its field list with `tags: &amp;TagIndex::empty(),` immediately before the closing brace, at lines 834, 916, 988, 1086, 1214, 1298, 1379, 1533'. Verified on disk: the 8th literal opens at line 1533 (`let input = CascadeInput {`), but its `tags` field is NOT `tags: &amp;TagIndex::empty(),` -- it is `tags: &amp;tags,` (a real, populated TagIndex built earlier in that test, at line 1542, not 1533). The other 7 sites (opening at 825/907/979/1077/1205/1289/1370) do follow the stated +9-line offset and literal text exactly. An implementer doing a literal find-and-insert-after `tags: &amp;TagIndex::empty(),` at line 1533 will find neither that text nor that field at that line, and may either fail the mechanical edit or insert `identity:` in the wrong place (e.g. right after the literal's opening brace instead of after its tags field).\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Correct the 8th site's citation in T2's step to: 'the 8th literal opens at line 1533; its `tags` field reads `tags: &amp;tags,` at line 1542; insert `identity: &amp;IdentityIndex::default(),` directly after that line.' Keep IdentityIndex::default() as the fill value (this fixture is not dual-identity), just fix the line number and field text so the instruction is mechanically followable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"T4\",\n      \"type\": \"under-specified\",\n      \"description\": \"T4's step 2/3 'RED (probe)' technique instructs reverting T3's fix, then running the two new fallback tests with the explicit expectation 'both pass even with the fix reverted' -- i.e., no failure occurs during this 'RED' step. This is a genuine and well-reasoned regression-invariance proof, but it directly conflicts with the hard rule stated in the lambda:implementer agent definition this plan will be executed by: 'Skipping the red phase is not allowed -- a test that passes before implementation indicates a broken test and the agent stops.' An implementer following that literal contract will halt on T4 believing the test is broken, even though the plan intends the pass-on-revert outcome as correct and documented. (T6's probe is fine by contrast -- its revert step genuinely produces a failing DependencyNotFound error, satisfying the standard RED gate.)\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Re-label T4's step 2/3 to make explicit that this task has no true implementation-side RED phase and is exempt from the fail-before-implement gate -- e.g. rename the step from 'RED (probe)' to 'REGRESSION-INVARIANCE PROOF (expected green on both sides -- do not treat pass-on-revert as a broken test; this task intentionally has no red phase)', or drop the revert/restore choreography entirely and just add the two fallback tests directly against the already-fixed code with a comment explaining why no red phase applies here.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"Both special-attention checks were run against live code. (1) The corrected design is implemented faithfully: Workspace gains an owned `identity: IdentityIndex` field populated via `graph.identity().clone()` inside the non-generic-over-D `impl&lt;'a, R: CommandRunner&gt; Workspace&lt;'a, R, ManifestWalkResolver&gt;` block (verified struct field at lib.rs:95, Ok(Workspace{...}) at 131-140), and CascadeInput takes `&amp;'a IdentityIndex` with real construction sites reading the plain struct field `&amp;ws.identity` (verified version.rs:73-83 tags line 82, snapshot.rs:54-64 tags line 63) -- no `ws.graph.identity()` method call reintroduced anywhere. (2) All 18 T1 Workspace-literal citations and all 7 (of 8) T2 in-file CascadeInput-literal citations, plus the 6 cascade_tests.rs citations, the T3 RewriteKey/eco citations, and the T6 version_tests.rs citations verified byte-exact against disk. One genuine drift found: T2's 8th cascade.rs literal (line 1533) is misdescribed -- its tags field is `tags: &amp;tags,` at line 1542, not `tags: &amp;TagIndex::empty(),` at 1533 as claimed, which will break the mechanical edit as literally written. Also found a genuine process-contract conflict: T4's revert-then-confirm-RED probe is explicitly documented to pass (not fail) during the 'RED' step, which collides with lambda:implementer's hard-coded stop-on-unexpected-green rule, and will likely halt execution. Both are concrete, fixable issues rather than nitpicks -- since this is presumably a first review round (no evidence of a prior round-2 context), both are treated as blocking. Coverage is otherwise complete: all 7 acceptance criteria (AC-001 through AC-007) appear in exactly one task's covers_criteria with no orphans, task ordering (T1-&gt;T2-&gt;T3-&gt;T4-&gt;{T5,T6}) is dependency-correct, and no compilation-boundary batching problems were found -- each task's own steps complete a full RED-IMPLEMENT-GREEN cycle before its commit.\"\n}\n```\n\nFiles verified: `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/lib.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/cascade_tests.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/version_tests.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/version.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/snapshot.rs`, `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs`, and the six T1 test files under `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/tests/`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>82460</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>24</tool_uses><duration_ms>231577</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
